## Architecture and delivery state machine

The broker inbox remains the source of truth. Never acknowledge/remove a
message merely because an app-server request was attempted. Track delivery by
stable c2c `message_id`; if an old local message lacks one, assign and persist a
stable ID before the first attempt rather than generating a new ID per retry.

Use an explicit state machine such as:

`Persisted -> Pending_injection -> Injecting -> Injected`

Recoverable paths return to `Pending_injection` with bounded exponential
backoff: server unavailable, authentication/reconnect failure, thread unloaded,
request timeout, transient protocol error, and process restart. Unsupported
schema/capability moves to `Fallback_pending`, where hooks or explicit polling
remain able to deliver the broker copy. Permanent malformed-message failure is
observable/dead-lettered without losing the original broker record.

Store an idempotency ledger keyed by `(managed identity, thread id,
message_id)`. Define atomic ordering between request completion and ledger
commit. Because a connection can fail after the server accepted the request but
before the response arrives, include `message_id` in the injected item's
machine-readable metadata/content envelope and reconcile loaded thread history
before retry when the protocol permits. If installed Codex offers no lookup or
deduplication mechanism, document and implement the safest at-least-once
behavior; do not claim exactly-once across the ambiguous-ack window.

Bound queue size/concurrency and preserve ordering for a single recipient.
Overload must leave messages durably pending, coalesce wake/reconnect work, and
emit a structured health/status signal rather than dropping or spawning
unbounded requests.

## Deliverables and receipt

- A focused ingress adapter module using T002's authenticated client/lifecycle
  seam, with no public CLI policy embedded in it.
- Durable idempotency/delivery-state storage plus fixture-gated tests for every
  state transition and failure window.
- Structured diagnostics/metrics sufficient for `doctor` or status integration
  in a later task: pending count, oldest age, retry count, fallback reason, and
  last protocol error, without message content or credentials.
- A tmux dogfood receipt showing two distinct messages and a retry of the same
  `message_id`, with sanitized app-server/broker evidence.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every inbound message is durably present in the canonical broker inbox before
  injection begins; killing the adapter at any instruction boundary cannot make
  the message unreadable from broker/fallback paths.
- The injected item preserves the c2c envelope and stable `message_id`, clearly
  identifies peer content as a c2c message/data item, and does not forge a local
  user approval or operator input.
- Re-delivering the same `message_id` during normal acknowledged retry produces
  one model-visible item. The ambiguous-ack crash case is explicitly tested and
  either reconciled to one item or documented/statused as at-least-once.
- Tests cover: clean delivery; duplicate enqueue; disconnect before request;
  disconnect after write/before response; authentication rejection; server
  restart; thread unloaded/resumed; unsupported method/version; malformed
  payload; queue overload; adapter restart with pending state; hook fallback;
  and ordered delivery of multiple messages.
- No code path invokes `turn/start`, `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`, PTY/tmux/
  Herdr submission, shell/filesystem app-server methods, or approval APIs.
- Literal peer content such as `allow`, `deny`, or a valid-looking approval
  token cannot create a verdict file or make `c2c await-reply` succeed.
- When app-server is unavailable or unsupported, the message remains queued and
  available to the existing Codex hooks/explicit poll path without duplicate
  deletion or archive corruption.
- Ephemeral messages retain their existing no-archive contract while remaining
  durable enough for the active delivery attempt; the task documents the exact
  crash/restart limitation if ephemeral durability cannot equal archived mail.
- Backpressure is bounded and observable; overload never drops messages or
  creates unbounded children/connections/retry loops.

## Verification commands

Run all focused adapter/idempotency/fault-injection tests plus:

```sh
just build
just check
./scripts/c2c_tmux.py list
```

Use `scripts/c2c_tmux.py` for the real remote-TUI dogfood. The receipt must
include broker pending state before injection, model-visible evidence after
injection, duplicate/reconnect evidence, and exact return codes with message
contents sanitized.

## Blockers

Blocked on P1.M1.E1.T001 and P1.M1.E1.T002. Do not proceed if T001 lacks a
positive protocol/control-boundary decision, if T002 does not expose an
authenticated client/lifecycle seam, or if stable thread identity cannot be
obtained. Composer-state feasibility does not block passive injection itself;
it blocks T007's automatic wake policy.
